---
// §3: who runs it, why it exists, and what it will not do. The ledger is the
// §16 out-of-scope list, stated as a promise rather than a roadmap gap.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, REPO_URL, SITE } from '../../consts';
import { t } from '../../i18n';
---

<BaseLayout title={t('about.title')} description={`Who runs ${SITE.title}, and why it exists.`}>
  <section class="page-head section">
    <p class="eyebrow">{t('about.eyebrow')}</p>
    <h1>A board of ideas nobody has to build.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The internet has enough serious people building serious products to solve serious problems.
      This is the other pile.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Who runs it</p>
      <h2>One editor, no committee.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-stack">
      <p>
        ProductUnhunt is run by {SITE.author}. The title is not real. The job is: read what people
        send in, publish what is funny and specific, and reject the pitch decks with the minimum of
        ceremony.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most of what is here at launch was written in-house. That is a starting condition, not a
        policy — the submission doors are open and the byline on a card is whoever thought the thing
        up.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Why it exists</p>
      <h2>The good part is the not-building.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-stack">
      <p>
        Every idea here cleared exactly one bar: someone thought about building it and chose not to.
        That decision is the most common event in the industry and the least documented one. Launch
        boards celebrate the shipping. Startup graveyards catalogue the dying. Nothing was keeping
        the receipts for the good calls.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the format is a straight face. Real editorial structure, real analysis, a status badge, a
        community verdict — applied to a product that does not exist and will not. The presentation
        is the joke. The restraint is sincere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-label={t('about.ledgerLabel')}>
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">The promises</p>
      <h2>{t('about.ledgerLabel')}</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="about-ledger">
      <article>
        <span>01</span>
        <h3>Build any of it</h3>
        <p>
          No incubator, no “we validated this in the comments”, no quiet spin-out of the one idea
          that turned out to be plausible.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span>
        <h3>Rank you</h3>
        <p>
          No ranking algorithm, no vote-driven featuring, no leaderboards. Newest first, and the
          filter row is the only sort there is.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span>
        <h3>Ask for your email</h3>
        <p>
          No accounts, no newsletter, no ads, and no tracking that follows you anywhere. Voting
          takes an anonymous cookie; the traffic counter takes nothing at all.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h2>The complete inventory.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-stack">
      <p>
        Casting a vote sets one first-party cookie: a random, signed, anonymous ID that exists so
        the same browser cannot vote twice, and expires after a year. Your browser also keeps a
        local note of which way you voted, purely so the buttons remember. Vote rate-limiting uses
        a salted hash of your IP address; the address itself is never stored. None of this happens
        until you actually vote — reading the site sets nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Traffic is counted with Umami, which is cookieless and aggregate: no persistent
        identifiers, no cross-site tracking, no personal data. That is the whole list, which is
        why there is no cookie banner — there is nothing to consent to.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose-grid about-closing">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Contact</p>
      <h2>Two doors.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-stack">
      <p>
        Ideas arrive as pull requests against the public repository, or as plain email. Corrections,
        complaints, and takedown requests use the same address.
      </p>
      <div class="action-row">
        <a class="button" href={withBase('/contribute/')}>{t('cta.submit')}</a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
        <a class="text-link" href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
